The video plays at normal speed but at a low frame rate (about 3 frames per second), so motion looks choppy and brief states can flash by or be skipped between frames — don't mistake low-framerate choppiness (a cursor jumping, a tooltip appearing in different spots, a half-drawn frame) for a rendering glitch. Inactive periods are cut, so the recording clock jumps forward across them. A footer at the bottom of every frame shows `REC_T:`, the whole number of seconds since the recording started; `WINDOW: X of Y`, which of the recording's browser tabs or windows is on screen, counted in the order they first appear; the `URL:` of the page in that window, without its query string and cut short when long; and `STATUS:`, which is `[ACTIVE]` during normal playback, `[IDLE]` on the frame where an inactive stretch is cut, and `[RECORDING ENDED]` after the last event. When the window number changes, the user switched tabs rather than navigated. Use `REC_T` to judge how long something lasted, never to say where it happened: positions are always in video time, counted from the start of the video file.
