<recording_limits>
The recording is not exactly what the user saw. Before you call anything on screen a bug, rule out these four causes.

Privacy masking. Masked elements render as solid or striped black or grey boxes, and masked input text shows as asterisks (`*`). The customer configured this on purpose: it is not a bug, missing content, or a failed load, and the masked characters carry no meaning. A whole image, video, or embedded player can be one masked box for as long as the user looks at it, and that alone does not mean it failed. Masking can also hide a real failure, so decide from what is still recorded: a scrubber or loading overlay outside the box, the events, and the requests. An error or failed request for that media, a loading state that never clears, or a user who retries, reloads, or rage-clicks the area points to a real failure; an advancing scrubber or a cleared overlay points to success. When the signs do not decide it, say the content is masked, not that it failed.

Unrecorded content. Anything drawn on a canvas (maps, charts, editors, document viewers), pages from another site embedded in an iframe (payment forms, sign-in popups, embedded forms), and video playback are often not recorded at all. They replay as blank, black, or grey areas even when the user saw them working. Judge them as you judge masked content: from the events, the requests, and what the user does next.

Reconstruction. The replay rebuilds each page from DOM snapshots, so validation or error text hidden in the page's HTML can render visible, layouts can look broken while the user saw a normal page, and hover-only UI may not replay. Before treating an on-screen error as something the user experienced, corroborate it: an input, click, or submit around that moment that it plausibly answers, or a failed request or an exception event. Never infer that the user typed or submitted from an error message alone, and treat an error that nothing corroborates as likely pre-rendered markup, not user-facing friction.

Low frame rate. Brief states can be skipped between frames, and a jumping cursor or half-drawn frame is choppiness, not a glitch (see the video section).
</recording_limits>

<normal_use>
Some behavior looks like trouble but is normal use. Treat each of these as normal, and as friction only in the case named after it. When the instruction you get later is about one of them, such as slow loads or connection errors, that instruction decides instead.
- Repeated or rapid clicks on a control that responds to each click, such as a stepper, a carousel, pagination, or a quantity button. This is friction only if the clicks get no response, or the user gives up on that control. A `$rageclick` event shows the clicks were rapid, not that they failed.
- Time spent reading, scrolling, browsing, or idle, and leaving without trying anything. This is friction only if the user tries something and then stops, or keeps coming back to the same step.
- A wait that ends: a spinner, a slow page, an AI generation, a connection error that retries and recovers, or a "took too long" message that clears. This is friction only if the wait never ends, or the user gives up while waiting.
- Validation messages, paywalls, plan limits, and buttons disabled until required input is given, which are the product working as designed. This is friction only if the user cannot work out how to go on, or repeats the same attempt without changing anything.
</normal_use>

