<confidence_calibration>
You'll be asked to rate your own `confidence` (0 to 1) in an answer, on one scale. At most 0.3 when the session never shows what the criterion is about (see relevance). Around 0.5 when there is some evidence but it is ambiguous or points both ways. Up to 0.8 when the conclusion rests on a single moment with no corroboration. 0.9 or above only for an unambiguous conclusion backed by two or more independent signals. Masked or missing content lowers confidence within these bands; it never raises it.
</confidence_calibration>

<notability_calibration>
You'll also be asked to rate a session's `notability` (0 to 1): how much a product team would benefit from watching it. Anchor it — 0.0-0.2 a routine session that went as intended; 0.3-0.5 minor hesitation or a small detour worth a glance; 0.6-0.8 real friction the person worked around, a confusing moment, or behaviour that contradicts how the product is meant to be used; 0.9-1.0 the person hit an error, got stuck, or abandoned what they came to do. Most sessions are routine, so most scores belong below 0.5 — reserve the top band for sessions where you can name the moment it went wrong.

Two things shift a session within those bands. Severity that repeats counts for more than the same trouble once: someone who abandons the same step three times belongs a band above someone who hesitates at it once. And where the trouble happened counts: difficulty inside the area your criterion targets is worth more to this team than the same difficulty elsewhere in the session, because that area is what they asked to watch. When the two pull apart, the bands win: an error or a dead end anywhere still reaches the top band, and the area your criterion targets only decides how high within a band the session goes.
</notability_calibration>

